      "scenarios": [
        {
          "lang": "en",
          "value": "AV:L - The vulnerable code is reached from an ordinary write(2) syscall (vfs_write → ext4_buffered_write_iter → file_modified → generic_update_time → __mark_inode_dirty), and the competing side requires local cgroup/writeback activity. No network protocol handler is involved.\nAC:L - The attacker drives both sides of the race — a multi-threaded write() loop dirties the inode while the attacker forces cgroup writeback switches by dirtying the file from a second memcg and tearing that cgroup down, making the old wb's last reference drop. Attempts are unlimited and the bug was observed occurring spontaneously during normal boot, and CONFIG_CGROUP_WRITEBACK is default-y on all mainstream kernels.\nPR:L - Only an unprivileged local account with write access to a file on a writeback-capable filesystem (ext4/xfs home or /tmp) is needed; cgroup manipulation used to widen the race is available to ordinary users through systemd's delegated per-user cgroup subtree.\nUI:N - The attacker triggers everything itself through its own write() syscalls and cgroup activity; no victim action or interaction is required.\nS:U - The use-after-free corrupts kernel heap memory within the same kernel security authority; there is no VM, IOMMU, or sandbox boundary crossed.\nC:H - The freed bdi_writeback is dereferenced as `wb->bdi->capabilities`, so a re-sprayed object yields an attacker-controlled pointer dereference and an arbitrary kernel-memory read primitive, enabling disclosure of arbitrary kernel data.\nI:H - wb_wakeup_delayed() writes into the freed object (spin_lock_irq on wb->work_lock) and inserts the freed `wb->dwork` timer into the timer wheel, giving list-write primitives and an attacker-controlled `timer->function` — a direct control-flow hijack path to arbitrary kernel code execution.\nA:H - The use-after-free reliably produces a kernel oops/panic as reported in the original crash trace (__mark_inode_dirty+0x124), and can be triggered repeatedly to crash the system."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
